## 1. SQL Schema (CREATE TABLE)

The memo table is defined in the `LATEST.sql` migration files for all three supported databases. The columns are identical in semantics, differing only in dialect-specific syntax:

**Files:**
- `/bench/home/work/store/migration/sqlite/LATEST.sql` -- line 33
- `/bench/home/work/store/migration/postgres/LATEST.sql` -- line 32
- `/bench/home/work/store/migration/mysql/LATEST.sql` -- line 32

**Columns (using the SQLite version as reference):**

| Column | Type | Notes |
|---|---|---|
| `id` | INTEGER PRIMARY KEY AUTOINCREMENT | System-generated ID |
| `uid` | TEXT NOT NULL UNIQUE | User-defined unique identifier |
| `creator_id` | INTEGER NOT NULL | Foreign key to the user table |
| `created_ts` | BIGINT NOT NULL | Unix epoch timestamp, defaults to now |
| `updated_ts` | BIGINT NOT NULL | Unix epoch timestamp, defaults to now |
| `row_status` | TEXT NOT NULL | CHECK constraint: `'NORMAL'` or `'ARCHIVED'`, defaults to `'NORMAL'` |
| `content` | TEXT NOT NULL | Memo content, defaults to `''` |
| `visibility` | TEXT NOT NULL | CHECK constraint: `'PUBLIC'`, `'PROTECTED'`, `'PRIVATE'`; defaults to `'PRIVATE'` |
| `pinned` | INTEGER NOT NULL | 0 or 1, defaults to 0 |
| `payload` | TEXT NOT NULL | JSON payload, defaults to `'{}'` (Postgres uses `JSONB`, MySQL uses `JSON`) |

There are also related tables `memo_relation` (lines 47-52) and `memo_share` (lines 102+) in each LATEST.sql.

